% Math mode demonstration. Grows one section per math-mode stage as it lands.
%   Stage 2 (done): inline $...$ — atom classes, inter-atom spacing, symbols.
%   Stage 3 (done): super/subscripts and the four math styles.
\def headline {\italic{Math mode demo} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{Math mode — Stage 2: atoms and spacing}

This document is typeset by \TeXish, whose math mode follows \TeX{} closely.
Inline mathematics is written between dollar signs and flows inside a line of
ordinary text, like $a + b = c$ or the inequality $x \leq y$, set in Latin
Modern Math through the glyph seam while the surrounding prose keeps to the
string seam.

\bold{Variables and Greek}

Latin and Greek letters are \italic{variables}, set in mathematical italic:
$x$, $y$, $z$, $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$, $\lambda$, $\mu$, $\pi$, $\omega$.
Capital Greek letters stay upright: $\Gamma$, $\Delta$, $\Theta$, $\Sigma$,
$\Omega$. A short formula mixes them freely: $\alpha x + \beta y = \gamma$.

\bold{Spacing follows the atom classes}

The space around a symbol depends on what kind of symbol it is. A binary
operator takes a medium space, $a + b - c$; a relation takes a thick space,
$a = b \neq c \approx d$; punctuation a thin space, $a, b, c$; and two
ordinary letters touch with none at all, $xy$ versus $x \cdot y$.

A binary operator that cannot really be binary is quietly reclassified, so a
sign reads tightly while a true operator gets its space: compare the unary
$-x$ and $+y$ against the binary $a - x$ and $p + q$, and note the minus in
$n = -1$ hugs its operand.

\bold{Operators, fences and sets}

Named functions are upright operators: $\sin x$, $\cos\theta$, $\log n$,
$\exp x$, $\lim$, $\max$, $\min$. Large operators print at their text size:
$\sum$, $\prod$, $\int$, $\oint$. Fences and sets behave too:
$(a + b)$, $[x]$, $\langle u \rangle$, $x \in S$, $A \subset B$,
$p \wedge q \vee r$.

\bold{Explicit spacing}

The author can tune spacing by hand with a thin space $a\,b$, a medium space
$a\:b$, a thick space $a\;b$, a negative thin space $a\!b$, and the wider
$a\quad b$ and $a\qquad b$.

\bold{Stage 3 — superscripts and subscripts}

A superscript is written with a caret and a subscript with an underscore, each
set smaller than the symbol it rides on: $x^2$, $a_i$, $x^2 + y^2 = z^2$. A single
character is taken as the script, so $x^2y$ scripts only the two; a braced group
scripts the whole thing, as in $e^{x + y}$ and $a_{i j}$.

An atom can carry both at once, kept clear of each other: $x_i^2$, $a_n^k$, and
the Riemann sum $\sum f(x_i)\,\Delta x_i$. Scripts nest, stepping down to
scriptscript size: $2^{2^{2^x}}$ and $a_{i_{j}}$.

Subscripts and superscripts read naturally on operators and variables alike:
the polynomial $a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + a_3 x^3$, the limit $\lim x_n = L$, a
power tower against a falling index in $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \cdots + x_n^2$, and a
bound integral $\int_a^b$.

\bold{Stage 4 — fractions}

A fraction sets a numerator over a denominator, stacked over a bar centered on
the math axis and set, inline, at script size: $\frac{1}{2}$,
$\frac{a + b}{c}$, $\frac{x^2 + 1}{x - 1}$. The parts are themselves math, so
they take scripts and nest: $\frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{x}}}$ and the
binomial-like $\frac{n!}{k! \, (n - k)!}$.

Fractions sit in running math like any other atom: the derivative
$\frac{dy}{dx}$, the slope $m = \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$, and a sum of
fractions $\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{3} = \frac{5}{6}$.

\bold{Stage 4 — radicals}

A square root sets a surd glyph, chosen tall enough to span its radicand, with
a bar drawn across the top. The radicand is set cramped, so
a superscript inside it sits a touch lower: $\sqrt{2}$, $\sqrt{x + 1}$,
$\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$. Radicals combine with everything else — they take scripts,
hold fractions, and nest: $\sqrt{a}^2$, $\sqrt{\frac{a}{b}}$, and
$\sqrt{1 + \sqrt{1 + x}}$.

A higher root carries a small index in the surd's kink: the cube root
$\sqrt[3]{x}$, the fourth root $\sqrt[4]{16} = 2$, the $n$th root
$\sqrt[n]{x}$, and an index that is itself an expression, $\sqrt[n+1]{x + 1}$.

The quadratic formula draws it all together:
$x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$.

\bold{Stage 5 — stretchy delimiters}

An ordinary delimiter is fixed in size, but a pair marked with \italic{left}
and \italic{right} grows to fit what it encloses. Around a tall fraction the
parentheses stretch to span it, $\left( \frac{a + b}{c + d} \right)$, and so do
brackets, $\left[ \frac{1}{x} \right]$, braces, $\left\{ \frac{n}{2} \right\}$,
and angle brackets, $\left\langle \frac{p}{q} \right\rangle$.

The fences scale to whatever they hold — a single symbol keeps them small,
$\left( x \right)$, while a nested radical pushes them taller,
$\left( \sqrt{\frac{a}{b}} \right)$. A delimiter may be left off one side with a
period, as in the absolute value $\left| \frac{x}{y} \right.$ or the cases-like
$\left\{ \frac{1}{2} \right.$, and floors and ceilings stretch as well:
$\left\lfloor \frac{x}{2} \right\rfloor$ and $\left\lceil \frac{n}{k}
\right\rceil$.

\bold{Stage 5 — accents}

An accent rides above the symbol it marks, centered on it and resting just above:
a hat $\hat{x}$, a bar $\bar{y}$, a vector $\vec{v}$, a tilde $\tilde{a}$, a dot
$\dot{x}$ and double dot $\ddot{x}$, an acute $\acute{e}$ and grave $\grave{e}$,
a check $\check{s}$ and a breve $\breve{u}$. Over a taller letter the accent
lifts to clear it: $\hat{b}$, $\vec{d}$, $\bar{l}$.

A wide accent stretches to span several symbols: $\widehat{x + y}$ and
$\widehat{ABC}$, $\widetilde{abc}$. Accents combine with the rest of math —
$\hat{x}^2$, $\vec{v} \cdot \vec{w}$, and $\bar{x} = \frac{1}{n} \sum x_i$.

\bold{Stage 5 — big operators with limits}

A large operator carries its bounds at its side inline, $\sum_{i=1}^{n}$ and
$\int_0^1$, but \italic{limits} stack them over and under and grow the operator:
the sum $\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n} i = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}$, the product
$\prod\limits_{k=1}^{n} k = n!$, and a union $\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{n} A_i$.

Named operators take limits too: the limit $\lim\limits_{x \to 0}
\frac{\sin x}{x} = 1$ and a supremum $\sup\limits_{n} a_n$. The integral keeps
its bounds at the side even with $\int\nolimits$, reading $\int_a^b f(x)\, dx$.

\bold{Stage 6 — display math}

A display is set on its own centered line, larger than the inline form, with the
big operators stacking their bounds automatically — no \italic{limits} needed.
Compare the inline geometric series $\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n} = 2$ with
its display:
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^n} = 2.$$
The fraction opens up and the sum grows. An integral keeps its bounds to the
side even in display, as it should:
$$\int_0^1 \frac{1}{1 + x^2}\, dx = \frac{\pi}{4}.$$
The quadratic formula, displayed, shows the radical stretching over the whole
discriminant:
$$x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.$$
A display can carry an equation number, flushed to the right margin with
\italic{eqno}, while the formula stays centered:
$$e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0 \eqno{(1)}$$
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\, dx = \sqrt{\pi} \eqno{(2.7)}$$

